===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|crime}} A [[code of silence]] amongst [[member#Noun|members]] of the [[Mafia]] that [[forbid]]s [[divulge|divulging]] [[insider]] [[secret#Noun|secrets]] to [[law enforcement]], often also [[follow#Verb|followed]] outside of the [[organization]] for [[fear#Noun|fear]] of [[retaliation]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Sicilian code}}
#: {{hypernyms|en|conspiracy of silence|wall of silence}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=7 November 1863|title=The State of Italy|journal=[[w:Public Opinion (magazine)|Public Opinion:{{nb...|A Comprehensive Summary of the Press throughout the World on All Current Topics}}]]|location=London|publisher=Cox and Wyman,{{nb...|printers, Great Queen Street, Lincoln’s-Inn Fields, London.}}|volume=IV|issue=III|page=520|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=RV9JAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA520|column=1|oclc=221064716|passage=Here we have the unfortunate heirloom of corrupt government—'''''Omertà''''' (a Sicilian word, meaning silence on the part of witnesses to a deed of blood). The Government arrests a delinquent and has him tried before a court; but the latter, for want of witnesses, cannot condemn. "We dare not speak," say the victims themselves; "the robber has powerful friends in these parts; we might be made to suffer worse than we have to complain of; it is better to bear with the past than to ruin the future."|footer={{small|Translated from ''Il Corriere Siciliano''.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864|author=w:Frances Power Cobbe|chapter=Italy Tried by Jury|title=Italics. Brief Notes on Politics, People, and Places in Italy, in 1864|location=London|publisher=[[w:Nicholas Trübner|Trübner and Co.]],{{nb...|Paternoster Row.}}|page=107|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/italicsbriefnote00cobbrich/page/107/mode/1up|oclc=457821148|passage=Where such a thing as Sicilian '''''omertà''''' exists, the most perfect laws, and the most honestly-intentioned executive in the world cannot escape falling into errors.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1877|month=August|author=T. Adolphus Trollope|title=The ‘Mafia’ and ‘Omertà’ in Society|editor=Sylvanus Urban [pseudonym]|journal=[[w:The Gentleman's Magazine|The Gentleman’s Magazine]]|location=London|publisher={{w|Chatto & Windus}},{{nb...|Piccadilly}}|volume=CCXLI|issue=1760|pages=160–161|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_gentlemans-magazine_1877-08_243/page/160/mode/1up|oclc=1071746321|passage=The power of the ''mafia'' in action is supported by a code of ethics, prevalent and exclusively respected throughout the island, called "'''''Omertà'''''." {{...}} '''''Omertà''''' requires that your own hand alone should protect your head; but it also requires that in any circumstances in which it should fail to be able to do so, the man who has '''''omertà''''' at heart must bend his head and suffer. Vengeance, however tardy, and obtained by whatever amount of treachery and striking from behind, is in honourable conformity with '''''omertà'''''; but there must be no appeal to law.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=29 July 1911|author=Ernesto Serao|title=The Truth about the Camorra|editor=Lyman Abbott|journal=[[w:The Outlook (New York City)|The Outlook]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=The Outlook Company|volume=98|issue=13|page=67|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_new-outlook_1911-07-29_98_13/page/725/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=5361126|passage=Among the social oaths dictated by the ''frieno'', by far the foremost in importance is the '''''omertà''''', the most solemn oath never to denounce, not even at one's deathbed, any wrong suffered through a fellow associate, never to reveal one's own murderer, never to have recourse to the aid of the law for any reason whatsoever, but to avenge the offense with one's own hands, if possible, after having first laid the complaint before the natural judges or the Camorra Tribunal.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 March 2005|author=Shelley Murphy|title=US seeking to seize Patriarca assets: Ex-mobster owes incarceration costs|journal=w:The Boston Globe|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=The Globe Newspaper Co.|volume=267|issue=63|section=City & Region section|page=B3|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-boston-globe-omerta/173543147/|column=1|issn=0743-1791|oclc=66652431|passage=Patriarca {{quote-gloss|{{w|Raymond Patriarca Jr.}}}} pleaded guilty in December 1991 to racketeering and conspiracy charges, but he refused to admit he was a member of the Mafia, clinging to his vow of "'''omerta'''" to the secret organization.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any [[code of silence]] (especially about something [[illegal#Adjective|illegal]] or [[secret#Adjective|secret]]), or [[refusal]] to [[talk#Verb|talk]] [[openly]] about something.
#* {{RQ:LATimes|date=27 October 2006|author=Ross K. Baker|title=Guns—the dead issue|page=A29|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-los-angeles-times-omerta/173543723/|columns=1–2|passage=There was a time that high-profile killings such as the 1968 assassinations of [[w:Robert F. Kennedy|Robert F{{quote-gloss|rancis}} Kennedy]] and {{w|Martin Luther King Jr.}} brought passionate cries for limitations on handguns. A bipartisan '''''omerta''''' now smothers the issue.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=7 February 2009|author=w:Mark Lawson|title=We're all in public now|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425112619/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2009/feb/07/surveillance-snitching-privacy-society|passage=But although her {{quote-gloss|{{w|Carol Thatcher}}'s}} agent has invoked that tradition of post-show '''omerta''' in her defence, the truth is that few would trust these days to what it is now possibly risky to call Chinese walls.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2016|month=October|author=w:Charles Seife|title=How the FDA Manipulates the Media [print version: How to Spin the Science News]|editor=w:Mariette DiChristina|journal=w:Scientific American|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250510094144/https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/how-the-fda-manipulates-the-media/|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Springer Nature|Springer Nature American]]|volume=315|issue=4|page=54|issn=0036-8733|oclc=473864361|passage=Of all the media outlets, the ''[[w:The New York Times|New York Times]]'' was the only one to mention the close-hold embargo: {{...}} The FDA {{quote-gloss|Food and Drug Administration}} was not pleased that the '''omertà''' had been broken.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=6 October 2023|author=w:Heather Stewart|quotee=w:Sadiq Khan|title=‘Choose London’: Sadiq Khan steps up efforts to lure EU citizens post-Brexit|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241203124541/https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/oct/06/london-sadiq-khan-eu-citizens-uk-capital-brexit-mayor|passage=Despite criticising what he {{quote-gloss|Sadiq Khan}} called an "'''omertà'''" in British politics about Brexit, he expressed some sympathy for the Labour leader [[w:Keir Starmer|Keir {{quote-gloss|Starmer}}]]'s wariness about the issue.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=21 February 2024|author=Kim Willsher|quotee=w:Judith Godrèche|title=Judith Godrèche to address French cinema's ‘omertà’ around #MeToo|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250416123642/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/feb/21/judith-godreche-to-address-france-silence-around-metoo-cesar-awards|passage=Judith Godrèche, an actor who has accused two high-profile directors of raping her as a teenager, will address France's most prestigious film awards ceremony on Friday in an unusual move aimed at breaking what she calls the "'''omertà'''" surrounding the abuse of women and girls in the industry.}}

